{
  "assessment": [
    {
      "Existence of an interference": "There was an interference with freedom of expression. The facts describe measures by the Russian authorities that “further curtail freedom of expression” through the closure of almost all remaining independent news organisations, severe restrictions on access to social media, and the resulting deprivation of information from independent sources (see paragraph 86). In addition, international monitoring found targeted killing of civilians including journalists, as well as unlawful detentions, abductions and enforced disappearances of such persons (see paragraph 118). Such measures affect both the individual right to impart information and ideas and the public’s right to receive them, which lies at the heart of Article 10. The Court has consistently treated restrictions on media outlets, journalists and access to information as interferences with Article 10 rights (see no. 5493/72; see no. 33348/96)."
    },
    {
      "Lawfulness of the interference": "The interference appears not to satisfy the requirement of being “prescribed by law”. The case summary contains no concrete domestic legal basis, no indication of clear and foreseeable rules, and no safeguards against arbitrariness for the closure of independent media, the blocking of social media, or the detention and disappearance of journalists (see paragraph 86; see paragraph 118). On the contrary, the material points to a broad crackdown aimed at suppressing independent reporting and dissent. As regards killings, abductions, enforced disappearances and unlawful detentions of journalists, those acts are by their nature incompatible with lawful regulation of expression (see paragraph 118). Where restrictions are sweeping, opaque and unsupported by accessible legal safeguards, the Court usually finds the lawfulness test unmet (see no. 6538/74; see no. 38224/03). This already strongly indicates a violation of Article 10."
    },
    {
      "Legitimate aims of the interference": "Even if one assumes that Russia would invoke national security, public safety, prevention of disorder, or the protection of the rights of others in the context of armed conflict, the facts do not show that the measures were genuinely directed to those aims in a Convention sense. The Parliamentary Assembly described the situation as one in which the Russian population was exposed only to State-controlled media amplifying a distorted narrative of the war (see paragraph 86). That description suggests viewpoint control and propaganda management rather than a narrowly tailored effort to protect a legitimate interest. Likewise, the targeting, detention or disappearance of journalists documented by international mechanisms (see paragraph 118) cannot plausibly be treated as serving a legitimate Article 10 § 2 aim. The Court gives States some margin in matters touching national security, but not for measures whose real effect is to eliminate pluralism and suppress independent scrutiny of public affairs, especially during war, when public oversight is particularly vital (see no. 9815/82; see no. 2668/07)."
    },
    {
      "Necessary in a democratic society": "The interference was not necessary in a democratic society. The material indicates a near-total suppression of independent media, serious restrictions on social media, and violent or coercive action against journalists (see paragraph 86; see paragraph 118). Those are extreme measures producing an obvious chilling effect, not the least restrictive means of addressing any legitimate security concern. No “relevant and sufficient” reasons are set out in the facts to justify shutting down almost all independent outlets or targeting journalists. The breadth and severity of the measures are especially problematic because the expression concerned relates to war, public affairs and human-rights reporting, where Article 10 protection is at its highest. The Court has repeatedly stressed that there is little scope for restrictions on political speech or on debate of matters of public interest, and that press freedom plays an essential “public watchdog” role (see no. 5493/72; see no. 33348/96; see no. 2668/07). In these circumstances, the interference cannot be convincingly defended as answering a pressing social need. The likely conclusion is that Article 10 was violated."
    }
  ],
  "violation": "yes"
}
